  "result": "Fixed. The bug was in `shop/storage.py:17`. `save_order` loads the existing rows, removes any old copy of the order being saved, and then appends the new row. But the filter was backwards: it kept only the rows whose id matched the new order (`==` instead of `!=`). So each save threw away every other order, and re-saving the same order left a duplicate. I changed it to `!=`, so saving now adds a new order or replaces the existing row for that id.\n\nI also added `tests/test_storage.py` with two tests:\n- **Two orders:** saving two different orders and then loading returns both.\n- **Re-save:** saving the same order again (after changing it to `paid`) leaves one row with the new state.\n\n**Test results:** the full suite passes (`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .`, 8 tests, OK). Both new tests fail when run against the original code, so they do catch this bug.",
